{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions","id":1112358,"title":"New member with some (probably noobish) questions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-06T14:50:47-07:00","id":1112358,"title":"New member with some (probably noobish) questions","content":"Hey guys! Thanks for the approval. My name is Joe, and I've got a couple years experience as a player in D&amp;D. I picked up my Ascension book years ago, intending on starting a group, but never had luck making that happen. Fast forward to now, the DM of my d&amp;d game is super frustrated with a few members of the group, who essentially devolved the game into a strict roll stats and grab loot game.<br /> <br /> Anyway, long story short, I've been asked to be the ST for mage. My DM wanted more RP elements, mage encourages to, and after the two sessions I've run (with 8 players), they want more. The current questions I can think of are these:<br /> <br /> 1. In combat, what is soak and how do i calculate it?<br /> 2. In an effort to keep RP prevalent in the game, I have my players explain their actions in combat. One example in my current group is a forces karate master. He wanted to hit a target with a melee flaming fist. I had him roll melee+forces, and made the dofficulty = his dice pool. Is that the correct way to calculate difficulty? Will dif always equal dice pool (not counting modifiers)?<br /> 3. In regards to damage, is there a general rule of thumb to calculating it?<br /> 4. As far as the flow of combat, is there a general order to it? I know there is an initiative order (alert + wits right?), but as far as on their turn, is there a specific order they are supposed to be performing? (Move, action, bonus action?)<br /> 5. Do players know they've gained paradox, or is it more interesting to keep that as a hidden stat that I can spring at any time?<br /> 6. Is it wrong of me as the ST to slightly punish those players who aren't really paying attention by increasing the difficulty of their rolls?<br /> <br /> I'm sure I'll have more questions, and I profusely apologize if these are answered any where in the core book or the book of shadows, or on the forum ahead of time. Thanks in advance for your help!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T15:59:43-07:00","id":1112389,"title":"","content":"*Blinks*<br /> <br /> *Tilts Head*<br /> <br /> ...have you...read the rulebook?<br /> <br /> I don't mean to sound condescending, I apologize. It's just that many of the questions are answered in any core rulebook Mage: The Ascension has released. The M20 quickstart guide is easiest to point to, since <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/131409/Mage-20th-Anniversary-Edition-Quickstart?src=hottest_filtered\" target=\"_blank\">you can download it for free</a> and is really compact.<br /> <br /> What book, exactly, are you reading from at the moment? What is it specifically? Is it a core book or a supplement? What edition is it? When does it say it was published?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T16:43:37-07:00","id":1112412,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1112389#post1112389\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*Blinks*<br /> <br /> *Tilts Head*<br /> <br /> ...have you...read the rulebook?<br /> <br /> I don't mean to sound condescending, I apologize. It's just that many of the questions are answered in any core rulebook Mage: The Ascension has released. The M20 quickstart guide is easiest to point to, since <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/131409/Mage-20th-Anniversary-Edition-Quickstart?src=hottest_filtered\" target=\"_blank\">you can download it for free</a> and is really compact.<br /> <br /> What book, exactly, are you reading from at the moment? What is it specifically? Is it a core book or a supplement? What edition is it? When does it say it was published?</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for the reply Bluecho<br /> <br /> My book has the ISBN # 1-56504-400-2<br /> Mage the Ascension, 2nd edition<br /> Most recent publish date I can find in the book is 1998<br /> <br /> If the answers to my questions are in the book, again, I apologize for asking dumb questions, I must have just misunderstood the explanation and will look for them again.<br /> <br /> **EDIT**<br /> <br /> Holy derpness, there is an entire section for combat in this book. Total noob maneuver on my part. Let me read this thoroughly and I will most likely answer my own questions. Sorry dude.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13642,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","user":"The3obaFett","date":". 07-06-2017, 07:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T16:57:55-07:00","id":1112417,"title":"","content":"To actually answer your questions, though:<br /> <br /> 1) If the damage taken by a character is of a type they are capable of soaking (mortals, by default, can only naturally soak Bashing damage), the character rolls their Stamina attribute (difficulty 6), with any Armor adding its rating to that pool. Each success on that roll subtracts health levels from the damage taken. If you get punched for five damage but roll three successes on soak, you only take two health levels of damage. For Lethal or Aggravated, mortal characters (which include mages, unless they have a Life 3 effect running that specifically lets them soak the aforementioned damage types) can only soak with their Armor rating, if any. If you get stabbed, and have no Armor, you take all the damage; if you had Armor of a rating of 1, you soak only with that 1 die.<br /> <br /> <br /> 2) Your player will make two rolls for this: an Arete roll to determine if the magickal Effect happens at all (and how potent it is), and a Dexterity + Martial Arts roll to determine if the character hits anything with it. I said Martial Arts because you specified that the character was using Karate, which would fall under the Martial Arts ability. They might alternatively use Brawl (for an informal fighting skill), or the Do ability (if the character is a member of the Akashayana/Akashic Brotherhood). Either way, the results of these two rolls determine what happens. If the character rolls no successes on Arete, the magic just doesn't happen. If they roll no successes on Dex + Martial Arts, the magic may happen, but they failed to connect with it. If both succeed, you calculate damage based on the Damage/Duration table; 2 health levels per success, with an extra success (and therefore 2 additional health levels) because the Effect involved Forces (damaging Effects that don't include Forces do not get this bonus, nor does this bonus apply if the roll generated no successes naturally).<br /> <br /> This general pattern applies to all attack Effects that need to be aimed as part of the character's Focus. Hand-to-hand attacks require Dex + Brawl/Martial Arts/Do, hand-held weapons require Dex + Melee, thrown weapon attacks require Dex + Athletics, and ranged weapon attacks usually require Dex + either Firearms or Archery or Energy Weapons. An Effect that targets a person directly or attacks an area skips this, and just rolls Arete. Pay attention not only to what the Sphere Effects are, but how the character enacts them through their Focus. An Akashic might throw a fire punch, an Etherite might discharge their flame thrower, a witch might generate a fireball in her hand and throw it, a Hermetic might point a wand and make fire erupt from the ground, and a Euthanatos might lock eyes with the enemy and cause them to spontaneously combust.<br /> <br /> <br /> 3) Damage from Magick should consult the Damage/Duration table, as specified above. Mundane attacks deal damage according to various ways, specific to how you dealt it. All of the dice pools from the previous paragraph apply here when it comes to determining a hit (difficulty dependent on what attack you use), and afterwards you use a damage dice pool determined by what caused it. Hand-to-hand, Melee, and thrown weapon attacks use Strength as the dice pool, plus any modifiers from the type of weapon. Guns have a fixed damage dice pool, and Energy Weapon attacks are...special cases. Since they're usually Magickal by nature, see the Damage/Duration table.<br /> <br /> <br /> 4) I don't think there's any mandate as to when a character can do which actions in their turn. If you have the movement or action(s) left, you can probably pull it off. There are no Bonus Actions in this game, though; you have an Action, half your max movement (or a full movement, if you forgo the action), and maybe some free actions like talking or the like. Combat turns in Mage last anywhere from 3 to 10 seconds. You CAN take more actions in a turn, but doing so forces you to divide your dice pools (take the lowest dice pool among the actions you'll do, and divide the dice between all the actions), unless you use Time 3 to make the character move faster.<br /> <br /> <br /> 5) While you can always keep the Paradox pool a secret if you really want to, there isn't much point in doing so. Whether you're using the 2ed/M20 rules or the Revised rules, the amount of Paradox points obtained is predictable and fixed. So long as you, the Storyteller, have answered two questions - &quot;Is the Effect Vulgar?&quot; and &quot;Are there Sleeper witnesses?&quot; - the players can always calculate how much Paradox they've obtained on their own. You don't roll to determine how many Paradox points a character receives; the value is fixed for the type of Effect used (or botched).<br /> <br /> <br /> 6) I think it IS unfair to punish players in that way. If players aren't paying attention, shouldn't you <i>talk to them</i> first, before making their characters suffer? Of course, it's your game, and you can do what you want. But this sounds like the sort of behavior that will create a hostile gaming environment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T17:39:04-07:00","id":1112432,"title":"","content":"I must throw it out there, this group I play with are my very best friends. The only reason I considered raising a roll difficulty on 1 particular person in the group is because he was blatantly screwing around, and doing to to be a pain in my arse lol. He pulls the same stuff in any other game we play, and I only wanted him to fail his roll for funny effects. <br /> <br /> I appreciate the information, I'll read the sections I obviously missed/forgot about and will return if I've more questions. (In my defence, I'm working on a double major in Math and Mechanical Engineering, so between running my company and being a student, I tried to read the core book in the wee hours of the morning, and woke up on more than a few occasions using the book as a pillow haha. Forgive my noobness)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T18:47:15-07:00","id":1112449,"title":"","content":"Storytelling mage for 8 players?<br /> <br /> Wow. My current almost year long game has had at most 6 players and, when they're all together, it's already a huge challenge to run a game. Keeping them all engaged while at the same time keeping track of what everyone is doing, where they're going, and what all the NPCs are up to... I tell you, I was at my limit.<br /> <br /> I honestly can't imagine running a game for 8 people at once.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-06T21:45:41-07:00","id":1112504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1112449#post1112449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Storytelling mage for 8 players?<br /> <br /> Wow. My current almost year long game has had at most 6 players and, when they're all together, it's already a huge challenge to run a game. Keeping them all engaged while at the same time keeping track of what everyone is doing, where they're going, and what all the NPCs are up to... I tell you, I was at my limit.<br /> <br /> I honestly can't imagine running a game for 8 people at once.</div> </div> </div> </div>Lol. Trust me when I say I didn't plan on there being 8 pc's. 2 of them were surprise add-ons. Last week we started the game, they happened to be at the house when we were getting ready to get underway, and asked if they could join in. I wasn't going to be a jerk and say no, but I didn't expect them to show up after the first session. They were the first two people this week to get a hold of me and ask if we were continuing haha.<br /> <br /> It's a struggle, which is what led to me ask about the punishments for not paying attention... with 8 people, the attention span of the group as a whole is like the same as a kindergartener lol.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T02:48:30-07:00","id":1112581,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The3obaFett</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions#post1112358\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey guys! Thanks for the approval. My name is Joe, and I've got a couple years experience as a player in D&amp;D.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey Joe! <br /> Welcome to storytelling mage, it's pretty hardcore - and you've got 8 players. Wow. Hats off to you. <br /> <br /> I think others have answered your questions above, but I just want to emphasise the core mechanics of wod games:<br /> <br /> Each player gets a dice pool to do stuff, usually stat+skill. They roll those dice against a certain difficulty, standard being 6+. Each 1 cancels a success. If they do that cancelling and still end up with a 1 left over, they botch. If they get one success after cancelling, it's a marginal success. If they get more than 1, improve the result accordingly. <br /> You can (and should) lower or raise the difficulty based on circumstances, and there's a whole load of difficulties listed for specific combat maneuvers in the combat section of the rulebook. <br /> Casting spells has a fixed difficulty based on the highest sphere in the rote and whether it's coincidental, vulgar or vulgar with witnesses, and uses arete as dice pool. <br /> Trying to do multiple tasks at the same time (two weapon wielding being the classic example) requires the player to split their dice pool between the two tasks. <br /> <br /> There's other aspects of the system like specialisms which add variation, and resonance and quintessence can modify the difficulty of spells, but that's more advanced. <br /> <br /> Regarding your massive mob of players, I'd heartily recommend you take a leaf out of the MET (larp) player's handbook, and recruit an assistant storyteller (ast). Work together to keep the players occupied and the story moving forward - you might enjoy creative sessions more with a partner as well :-) <br /> That way you can be working with half the group at a time each, or have sub-groups on side plots without losing flow.<br /> <br /> Edit: actually the way multiple actions work depends on the version of the rulebook you have - so check it thoroughly!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13587,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13587-eyrelliah","user":"Eyrelliah","date":". 07-07-2017, 06:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13587-eyrelliah","name":"Eyrelliah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T06:36:35-07:00","id":1112640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eyrelliah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1112581#post1112581\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey Joe! <br /> Welcome to storytelling mage, it's pretty hardcore - and you've got 8 players. Wow. Hats off to you. <br /> <br /> I think others have answered your questions above, but I just want to emphasise the core mechanics of wod games:<br /> <br /> Each player gets a dice pool to do stuff, usually stat+skill. They roll those dice against a certain difficulty, standard being 6+. Each 1 cancels a success. If they do that cancelling and still end up with a 1 left over, they botch. If they get one success after cancelling, it's a marginal success. If they get more than 1, improve the result accordingly. <br /> You can (and should) lower or raise the difficulty based on circumstances, and there's a whole load of difficulties listed for specific combat maneuvers in the combat section of the rulebook. <br /> Casting spells has a fixed difficulty based on the highest sphere in the rote and whether it's coincidental, vulgar or vulgar with witnesses, and uses arete as dice pool. <br /> Trying to do multiple tasks at the same time (two weapon wielding being the classic example) requires the player to split their dice pool between the two tasks. <br /> <br /> There's other aspects of the system like specialisms which add variation, and resonance and quintessence can modify the difficulty of spells, but that's more advanced. <br /> <br /> Regarding your massive mob of players, I'd heartily recommend you take a leaf out of the MET (larp) player's handbook, and recruit an assistant storyteller (ast). Work together to keep the players occupied and the story moving forward - you might enjoy creative sessions more with a partner as well :-) <br /> That way you can be working with half the group at a time each, or have sub-groups on side plots without losing flow.<br /> <br /> Edit: actually the way multiple actions work depends on the version of the rulebook you have - so check it thoroughly!</div> </div> </div> </div>Slight mistake regarding the botch rules there.<br /> <br /> You Botch if you roll no successes and one or more 1s.<br /> That means that Willpower expenditure to buy a success eliminates the possibility of a Botch.<br /> <br /> The only way of botching if you rolled successes is if they all get taken away by the Treshold rule.<br /> (Treshold occurs mostly if modifiers take the difficulty over 9)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T08:40:31-07:00","id":1112702,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nonsense</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1112640#post1112640\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Slight mistake regarding the botch rules there.<br /> <br /> You Botch if you roll no successes and one or more 1s.<br /> That means that Willpower expenditure to buy a success eliminates the possibility of a Botch.</div> </div> </div> </div>But! <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett\">The3obaFett</a> is using a <i><b>2nd Edition</b></i> hardcopy of Mage, where the old botch rule of 'more 1's than <i>'successes is a botch'</i> is still correct <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Which is my biggest worry in this whole thread, really. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"13642\" data-vbnamecard=\"13642\">The3obaFett</a> I seriously suggest not to start your sessions with that old edition. Many things got improved, clarified, and many people here mostly have played either Revised, or are now using the very new Mage 20th Anniversary edition, so getting forum help would be also a lot easier with those newer editions.<br /> <br /> I really, really suggest either getting a hardcopy and PDF of Revised or M20. Both are good editions, with the big divide between uses of one of the other boiling down to some rules and fluff preference.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 07-07-2017, 11:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T08:53:03-07:00","id":1112709,"title":"","content":"Or, again, you could take a look at the free M20 quickstart book I linked to earlier. Just to grasp what you're getting into, before you make the decision to invest in the M20 core book. The latter is a real doorstopper. That said, I think it can be worth the investment. Not only does it streamline a number of mechanics, it updates the setting for the modern age. And the chapter on Focus is worth the price of admission all on its own.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 07-07-2017, 11:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T09:35:29-07:00","id":1112737,"title":"","content":"I agree in all points.<br /> <br /> Also, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett\">The3obaFett</a> , a thing that I cannot stress enough is..if your players are coming from D&amp;D and are used to be the hardcore monster slayer heroes...well. You can play Mage like that, certainly. But a point you need to drive home either way is: Mages are humans. Most Mages were normal, everyday humans with friends, family, a job, and a very normal life until they suddenly awakened.<br /> <br /> They got raised by normal parents, went through normal school, had a First Childhood Crush, were possibly bullied (or possibly were a bully..), might or might not be in college debt or be struggling to move up in the world ..all the normal sins, quirks, normalicies and things that come with growing up in a n oh so mundane spot.<br /> <br /> Not *every* Mage of course - there are oddballs, gutter rats, members of fringe cultures, children that grew up in Mage-tainted surroundings (or were downright children of Mages..which does *not* automatically make them awaken!), but on the average, a modern Mage had a modern, normal life before.<br /> <br /> The reason I am making this very point is, that Mage is heavy on the <i>'What does it mean to be human?'</i> and <i>'What would a normal person do if they could suddenly do Magic?'</i> questions. Don't get me wrong - old Mages can be downright alien and inhuman. Mentally and physically. But a big amount of the Mage's life, and possibly to the end, they carry around all the baggae that comes with a normal upbringing.<br /> <br /> And this includes having to deal with all that happens in your head when you might be, suddenly, forced to hurt or kill another human being. And being a Mage in the World Of Darkness, that moment comes sooner than later.<br /> <br /> <br /> An alternative to all that would be to look in the direction of &quot;Dark Ages: Mage&quot;, or &quot;Mage: The Sorceror's Crusade&quot;, two WoD Mage gamelines that focus on the Dark Ages and the beginning of the Renaissance in the case of Sorceror's Crusade. The latter (Sorceror's Crusade) also uses the Magick system that the modern day Mage edition uses, and has a lot of sourcebooks.<br /> &quot;Dark Ages: Mage&quot; only has two books, but it has a very interesting take on how the Magick system mechanics work between the individual Mage groups - some people love it.<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> And just to add: What I wrote does not mean that the modern day Mage games can't be epic adventures. Oh, they can be epic. Grand. Weird, dark, utterly violent and/or deciding over the very fate of the game world. They can be as small and personal, or as reality shaking as you can possible imagine...<br /> I just wanted to point out where most Mages, before their awakening, are coming from <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 07-07-2017, 12:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T11:39:07-07:00","id":1112809,"title":"","content":"When it comes to crafting a story, I'd recommend the following steps.<br /> <br /> 1) Think about the kind of story you want to tell. This can be as grand as searching for 5 lost talismans that have been scattered across the world, to a simple story of finding a lost child's mother. Heck, you can even steal from shows or books you like. Mage can work really well in all kinds of plots. As long as you have an idea of a story you want to tell, you're good.<br /> <br /> 2) Ask your players what kind of story they want to play. This can be done individually or as a group. I usually recommend to do this one on one, but with 8 players, that would be quite the undertaking. If they have a basic idea for characters, or already have fleshed out characters, talk to them about this.<br /> <br /> 3) See where their contributions can be incorporated into your story, which elements match up, whether certain NPCs can actually be combined into the same person.<br /> <br /> 4) Add new stuff or refine old stuff to fill in the gaps.<br /> <br /> <br /> As an example, I'll use my Keepers of the Wyck campaign.<br /> <br /> 1) Marauder running about town who believes in HP Lovecraft's world, causes breaks in the gauntlet, a lot of different factions want to stop the tears, but no one actually knows what's causing it yet.<br /> <br /> 2) I had 6 players, and they each wanted different stories. In no particular order they wanted: A spy/revenge story, a Film Noir, a verbena story with a personal quest to search for King Arthur, a combat focused story, and a story about screwing over the Technocracy with cyber thrills. The sixth player wasn't sure what kind of story they wanted, and were happy just to be a Verbena teacher in this world.<br /> <br /> 3) The factions I had in mind for the marauder were a Euthanatos mage, some Void Engineers, the NWO, A group of nephandi (who want to use them) and the marauder's sister, who just wants to find him. Noir detective could be hired by the sister, and have a Euthanatos femme fetale on their tail, and there was plenty of opportunity to screw the technocracy over on their investigations. Combat could be easily done by having a lot of spirits falling through the holes of the gauntlet.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, I've got two verbena people, and one person who wants a revenge story. Perhaps they want revenge against a verbena person, linking these people up.<br /> <br /> 4) I'll need to make a Verbena coven for the Verbena people to join, and have the person the Revenge person's target be one of the high acting members. Also, I can make their spy story work by letting them pretend to be Verbena in order to investigate them on behalf of their own tradition.<br /> I then made this coven up, the NPCs for it, and called them the Keepers of the Wyck. How do they tie into the Marauder stuff? Quite simply, they are worried about all the spirits that are being flung into the natural world. Not strong enough? I can make one of the nephandi an ex member of the coven.<br /> <br /> And there we go, got some connections started, got a setting underway, all I have to do now is start it off and adjust things depending on what the players do with it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T15:22:44-07:00","id":1112953,"title":"","content":"Thanks for all the feedback so far guys!<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6156_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6156\" data-vbnamecard=\"6156\">Bluecho</a> that M20 Quickstart guide is gold. Thank you for the information.<br /> <br /> The consensus seems to be I oughtta get into the M20 world. I didn't really have an idea that the different versions were all that different. I have no one locally, including my local game shops, that even knew what Mage was, let alone be a resource I could ask questions to. <br /> <br /> I started the game actually a couple of months before we all ever got together and even rolled a single die. My d&amp;d DM was tired of our group turning his game into a dungeon crawl/stat game, and he asked me what we should do about it. (I assist him in our D&amp;D game, so if there is ever a rule question or something of the sort, he doesn't have to stop what he is doing to look it up, that's my job). I told him about Mage, and how it focuses mostly on roleplaying and not necessarily as much on stats and rolls. He liked the idea of pausing our D&amp;D game to try something new, as he was at his wits end and ready to just give up DM'ing all together.<br /> <br /> I had everyone who had interest in playing Mage think of a character background, with the limits that they were all students or associated with a fictional private university set in the Massachusetts area. I left the rules of Mage vague to them on purpose; 3 of the people in my group are hardcore powergamer types, always looking to min/max stats. Those same three even commented that they wished they knew the rules better so they could plan accordingly, but they also realized I was vague to avoid that very thing. I ended up with the following:<br /> <br /> 1. A &quot;world class&quot; Magic the Gathering player, who's parents throw money at the university to keep him there<br /> 2. A &quot;hipster&quot; type, who is a germaphobe, vegan, and is afraid of conflict<br /> 3. An orphaned girl who's family comes from wealth, her parents died in a boating accident, and she is honoring their wishes of becoming a brain surgeon<br /> 4. A jock type, who was recruited due to his incredible ability to throw a football, and who's father has a military background<br /> 5. An awkward computer nerd<br /> 6. A karate blackbelt who is an Economics major<br /> 7. A janitor at the school who got into some trouble in the past, is on probation, and is working as a janitor because it was the first opportunity to get a legit job<br /> 8. A socialite named Gerard Butler (yes, like the actor)<br /> <br /> So far in their story, they've met each other, their future mentor, and a friendly NPC (who is also a student at the university, and also moonlights as the Bartender at the local watering hole), and have gone from sleepers to &quot;glowies&quot; (not sure I'd consider them fully awoken yet?). They were presented with 9 items, and asked to choose the one that spoke to them the most. Each item, of course, is tied to one of the 9 traditions, and unbeknownst to them, are an &quot;ancient foci&quot; passed down through the traditions, choosing them akin to how the Green Lantern's ring chose him.<br /> <br /> Their first encounters will be with technocratic type bad guys. The school they attend is a recruiting ground for them. I really didn't plan to run an incredibly long story from them, but from the feedback I've gotten from my players, they all want to continue this for quite a while. They really aren't helpful as far as story input, they weren't so much when we played d&amp;d, I think they're more just along for the ride.<br /> <br /> So long story short, I need to look into the 20th anniversary edition of the core book for the sake of my sanity (and streamlining of some of the mechanics and rules), I should have said no to the 2 additional players, and I must remember that Mage's are people, and not d&amp;d monster slayers. (Also, all the rule clarifications are extremely helpful, and I can't thank you guys enough for the answers so far.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T16:06:27-07:00","id":1112979,"title":"","content":"This is looking pretty positive.<br /> <br /> If I were in your place, I'd have a sit down with these powergamers of yours and explain to them in plain terms that Mage's magic system is an extremely easy system to break. If they try to powergame it, then they will be able to do so very easily, and as a result destroy the game entirely. I would say to them that this is not the kind of game that can be played like this, and urge them to not think about levelling up or developing a good build, but just thinking about the story they want to tell and the journey of their character's arc. If they then told me that they wouldn't have any fun if they didn't power game it, I'd say that we have too many people anyway, so it might be a better idea for them to sit this one out.<br /> <br /> Personally, I would recommend reading Revised as a starting point. M20 has a lot going for it, but the book is over 700 pages long. Also, I honestly think Revised explains there spheres a heck of a lot better than M20 does, which is light on examples and tends to assume you already understand how the spheres work from previous editions.<br /> <br /> This is an interesting number of characters you've got here. Just so you know, being awakened is a pretty binary thing. Either their avatar is awake and allowing them to do magic, or it isn't.<br /> <br /> Personally I think it's a shame you didn't give them a chance to chose a tradition for themselves as a tradition can really help one figure out their paradigm/focus as well as how they view the universe and magic in general. Having their tradition be thrust upon them by a random choice of object feels a bit like choice taken away from the player. But it's your game, so I can't really say anything. Perhaps your game will be about the struggle between free choice and destiny.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T18:32:43-07:00","id":1113031,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1112979#post1112979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is looking pretty positive.<br /> <br /> If I were in your place, I'd have a sit down with these powergamers of yours and explain to them in plain terms that Mage's magic system is an extremely easy system to break. If they try to powergame it, then they will be able to do so very easily, and as a result destroy the game entirely. I would say to them that this is not the kind of game that can be played like this, and urge them to not think about levelling up or developing a good build, but just thinking about the story they want to tell and the journey of their character's arc. If they then told me that they wouldn't have any fun if they didn't power game it, I'd say that we have too many people anyway, so it might be a better idea for them to sit this one out.<br /> <br /> Personally, I would recommend reading Revised as a starting point. M20 has a lot going for it, but the book is over 700 pages long. Also, I honestly think Revised explains there spheres a heck of a lot better than M20 does, which is light on examples and tends to assume you already understand how the spheres work from previous editions.<br /> <br /> This is an interesting number of characters you've got here. Just so you know, being awakened is a pretty binary thing. Either their avatar is awake and allowing them to do magic, or it isn't.<br /> <br /> Personally I think it's a shame you didn't give them a chance to chose a tradition for themselves as a tradition can really help one figure out their paradigm/focus as well as how they view the universe and magic in general. Having their tradition be thrust upon them by a random choice of object feels a bit like choice taken away from the player. But it's your game, so I can't really say anything. Perhaps your game will be about the struggle between free choice and destiny.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Good idea about having a chat with my power gamers. They will eventually figure out how to build some power toons, they always do in any game we play, hopefully they realize it takes the fun out of the game if they do. And if they don't, then, I will deal with it when that crossroad comes I guess lol.<br /> <br /> Like I said in a previous post, I wasn't exactly planning on having this be a game that lasted more than a couple of weeks. (The intention was to remind them that roleplaying is fun, and my D&amp;D DM needed a bit of a break. His idea was we play mage, and hopefully some of that RP style sticks with them when we resume the game. We're 9 months into his game, so he didn't want to abandon it, but at the end of last Mage session, he expressed that he was cool if we took a very extended break from D&amp;D, and the rest of the group agreed).<br /> <br /> I may end up still cutting this game short, and chalk it up to a tutorial. As no one else in this group had heard of Mage before, and I don't have a complete grasp on every aspect of it, I didn't want to shotgun blast them with all the possible information. After they learn a little about the traditions, I can always ret-con or change who they might naturally have an affinity with. Or, something catastrophic happens, there's a party wipe, and we can start a fresh game with new toons (and hopefully a smaller group), and they will be a little more informed of the game mechanics and information. (I'm not saying I'm going to kill them off, but I will discuss with the group if that's an avenue they want to go). Then again, we did that with D&amp;D, and I absolutely loved the very first toon I made (a tiefling warlock named Augut who had an unnatural attraction to shiny objects, and ended up jumping into a portal, doomed to forever fall through the Elemental Plane of Air, lmao), and it took a little while to get back into D&amp;D after such an event.<br /> <br /> Also, why is the M20 book over 700 pages?!?!!? What did they add to it to make that happen?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T18:36:40-07:00","id":1113032,"title":"","content":"You could, alternatively, decide that the previous two sessions were a &quot;trial run&quot;, and now the players get to create new characters if they so choose, while being given a greater understanding of the rules, the Traditions, and the Focus system. If you do get M20's core book, you might benefit from copying the sections for Focus, the Spheres, and the Traditions into separate PDF files or a print-out, so the players can read through them.<br /> <br /> But all of that assumes you want to go to the extra work. Do what makes sense for you.<br /> <br /> If you DO decide to start over, now would be a good time to sit down with the group and see if anyone would mind sitting the game out. Emphasizing how doing so would lower the burden on you, and that taking one for the team would be greatly appreciated.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T18:52:01-07:00","id":1113043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The3obaFett</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1113031#post1113031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, why is the M20 book over 700 pages?!?!!? What did they add to it to make that happen?</div> </div> </div> </div>Some of it has to do with the long-winded and informal writing style of the book's lead writer, Phil Bruccato. He's been writing for the game since 2nd edition, and was most enthusiastic to helm the 20th anniversary book.<br /> <br /> But the other part is simply because they tried to cram in as much material as they felt was relevant to the game. Which meant drawing upon dozens of details from dozens of books published between the start and end of MtAs. Conditioning rules from Guide to the Technocracy, overviews of the Umbra's various realms and how to travel them, rules for Certamon challenges from the Order of Hermes, write-ups for three Alternate Spheres (Data, Primal Utility, and Dimensional Science) as written in the Revised Technocracy Convention books, detailed overviews of the various Sects that are major sides in the Ascension War (including turning the various Crafts into a semi-formal Sect, where none had existed before), both primary and secondary Abilities (the latter including stuff like Animal Ken, Flying, Energy Weapons, and Do), etc. Not to mention brand new material, like the aforementioned Focus section; sure, we'd had lists of potential Instruments (&quot;Foci&quot;) before, but not broad descriptions of Paradigm or Practice, which had only been talked about piecemeal. While the game always told us <i>what</i> tools a mage could use for their magic (Instruments), M20 goes more into detail into <i>how</i> those tools are used (Practice), and <i>why</i> the mage uses them that way (Paradigm).<br /> <br /> While the book could probably be more concise (and could use an editor to trim some of the fat), it IS still a dense read, trying to include a lot of material that previously filled out other books. And believe it or not, this isn't even all the material they planned to include. M20 had to be chopped up into two or three other books, only one of which (How Do You Do That?, a primer one Sphere mechanics that should be taken with a grain of salt) has yet been released. The core book, though, contains the essentials, and reading through the 2nd and Revised core books should fill out anything that M20 misses, so long as you're willing to integrate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 07-07-2017, 09:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T18:57:19-07:00","id":1113045,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1113032#post1113032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could, alternatively, decide that the previous two sessions were a &quot;trial run&quot;, and now the players get to create new characters if they so choose, while being given a greater understanding of the rules, the Traditions, and the Focus system. If you do get M20's core book, you might benefit from copying the sections for Focus, the Spheres, and the Traditions into separate PDF files or a print-out, so the players can read through them.<br /> <br /> But all of that assumes you want to go to the extra work. Do what makes sense for you.<br /> <br /> If you DO decide to start over, now would be a good time to sit down with the group and see if anyone would mind sitting the game out. Emphasizing how doing so would lower the burden on you, and that taking one for the team would be greatly appreciated.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I have no problems doing extra work, and I was actually planning on printing off information for my players soon enough. Within the next wekk or so, I'll have them decide if they want to continue, start a new game with their new knowledge about the game, or go back to D&amp;D (DM depending). I don't see D&amp;D being a viable avenue, and if we restart, it may discourage my addon players to bow out, which sounds kind of bad, but I do kind of hope happens.<br /> <br /> Is drivethru rpg the best place to get ahold of books? I don't mind PDF's, but I'm a hard copy for backup, post-it-notes everywhere kind of guy.<br /> <br /> I was also thinking, which every person who's ever been a DM says not to do, of splitting the party. Now before you object, running 2 groups of 4 is way, way more manageable than 1 group of 8. I can keep the pace up, and it'll be easier to coordinate schedules. I can also have them exist in the same realm, and same story, possibly playing for the same side, or different sides, and on one Sunday when we can all meet, have the story conclude by having an all out battle between the two groups. The game is new enough that it could be possible to pull off, and if i can weave some intrigue into the story enough, and sow some distrust, they might naturally want to separate. (My powergamers always butt heads, and we have played split d&amp;d in the past, so it's not necessarily an improbable avenue given the group I'm dealing with). Do any of you have experience in pitting your players against each other in that manner?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T19:03:12-07:00","id":1113046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The3obaFett</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1113031#post1113031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, why is the M20 book over 700 pages?!?!!? What did they add to it to make that happen?</div> </div> </div> </div>Two words: Meta Plot.<br /> <br /> M20 had the burden of having to go over pretty much everything that had been brought up in every book that came before it. This includes cliff notes of all the meta narratives, the transformation of the technocracy, the rogue council, the avatar storm, information on every possible faction inlcuding all the traditions, crafts, Technocratic conventions, and a (in my opinion, pretty poor) overview on the nephandi and marauders. Oh, and there's also all the little rules and systems that cropped up over the years, like the arts of Do, True Faith, Alternative history options, and combat rules, oh so many combat rules, and tables! About 6 pages worth of tables, and that's not even getting into the magic side of things.<br /> That being said, it still hasn't got everything. It very deliberately does not include any information about the unawakened Sorcerers, for example.<br /> <br /> Also, Mage books have a horrible habit of explaining things at least twice (sometimes three times) in different ways, often with each part omitting something mentioned somewhere else. Usually the first time is the narrative bit which talks about things in context to the world, while the later explanation are more mechanics based. But not always, as many important mechanics will be mentioned in the narrative bit but completely omitted from later mentions. M20 does this in spades, some things requiring you to jump all over the book before you get the complete picture.<br /> <br /> It's a terrible introduction to Mage, but as a compendium, it's not too bad.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T19:19:03-07:00","id":1113053,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The3obaFett</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1113045#post1113045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have no problems doing extra work, and I was actually planning on printing off information for my players soon enough. Within the next wekk or so, I'll have them decide if they want to continue, start a new game with their new knowledge about the game, or go back to D&amp;D (DM depending). I don't see D&amp;D being a viable avenue, and if we restart, it may discourage my addon players to bow out, which sounds kind of bad, but I do kind of hope happens.<br /> <br /> Is drivethru rpg the best place to get ahold of books? I don't mind PDF's, but I'm a hard copy for backup, post-it-notes everywhere kind of guy.</div> </div> </div> </div>The beauty of Drive Thru RPG is that you can go both ways. You can buy just a PDF, get a book printed on demand, or even have them both. Of course, M20 being such a large book means that printing it will be expensive. So do what makes the most sense for your budget, keeping in mind that the PDF is the cheapest (and fastest, since you won't need to wait for the book to be delivered in the mail). The PDF is also the easiest to make copies from, since it's just a copy+paste away. I'd recommend only committing to the physical book if you like what you read in the PDF and are satisfied enough to make that investment.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The3obaFett</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1113045#post1113045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was also thinking, which every person who's ever been a DM says not to do, of splitting the party. Now before you object, running 2 groups of 4 is way, way more manageable than 1 group of 8. I can keep the pace up, and it'll be easier to coordinate schedules. I can also have them exist in the same realm, and same story, possibly playing for the same side, or different sides, and on one Sunday when we can all meet, have the story conclude by having an all out battle between the two groups. The game is new enough that it could be possible to pull off, and if i can weave some intrigue into the story enough, and sow some distrust, they might naturally want to separate. (My powergamers always butt heads, and we have played split d&amp;d in the past, so it's not necessarily an improbable avenue given the group I'm dealing with). Do any of you have experience in pitting your players against each other in that manner?</div> </div> </div> </div>As a person who has never had the pleasure of GMing a game before, I can't personally say whether splitting the party is a good or bad idea. I'll leave that to the more experienced folk on the forum to field. That said, it certainly sounds more manageable to break up the large group into two smaller chunks. Each one can be focused on those present, giving players a greater time to shine and explore the roleplaying aspects of the game.<br /> <br /> It would also let you experiment more with the allegiances of the characters than you would otherwise. If the players are amenable, one group might be Tradition mages, while the other are Technocracy or Disparate ones. This would lend itself to an eventual confrontation, since they belong to different sides in the Ascension War. If you're handing out info from the book anyway, gauge how receptive certain players are to playing Technocrats or Disparates.<br /> <br /> Then again, circumstances may cause individuals from different sides to work together. Certain Disparate Crafts have been known to fraternize with Traditions that are similar, to the point that Revised actually folded a number of Crafts into the Traditions. M20 is metaplot agnostic by and large, so you could make this change in your setting true in whole, only in part (some members of a Craft went to the Traditions, while others remained independent), not true at all, or have membership overlap or be feigned (a Craft mage might play at being loyal to the Traditions, while secretly also working for the Disparate alliance). On the Technocrat side, agents of the Union might be working with Tradition mages for their own reasons - either against Union policy or under Union orders. They might even be spies and double agents within the sect's ranks, claiming membership in the Traditions while funneling intel to the Union and occasionally sabotaging Tradition efforts from within. As the characters grow closer, they may even be forced to examine where their priorities and loyalties lie: with the Union or with their friends. Defection or triple crosses may arise, if the players choose to pursue those avenues.<br /> <br /> On yet another hand, the two gaming groups may both be in the same sect, but be rivals. I'm imagining two Chantries, either in the same city or in neighboring cities, depending on how separated you want the groups to be in setting (at first).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T19:29:20-07:00","id":1113057,"title":"","content":"Splitting the party: Twice the work with half the time.<br /> <br /> Only recommended for GMs who want to do nothing but think about mage during their free time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T20:08:29-07:00","id":1113070,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1113046#post1113046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Two words: Meta Plot.<br /> <br /> M20 had the burden of having to go over pretty much everything that had been brought up in every book that came before it. This includes cliff notes of all the meta narratives, the transformation of the technocracy, the rogue council, the avatar storm, information on every possible faction inlcuding all the traditions, crafts, Technocratic conventions, and a (in my opinion, pretty poor) overview on the nephandi and marauders. Oh, and there's also all the little rules and systems that cropped up over the years, like the arts of Do, True Faith, Alternative history options, and combat rules, oh so many combat rules, and tables! About 6 pages worth of tables, and that's not even getting into the magic side of things.<br /> That being said, it still hasn't got everything. It very deliberately does not include any information about the unawakened Sorcerers, for example.<br /> <br /> Also, Mage books have a horrible habit of explaining things at least twice (sometimes three times) in different ways, often with each part omitting something mentioned somewhere else. Usually the first time is the narrative bit which talks about things in context to the world, while the later explanation are more mechanics based. But not always, as many important mechanics will be mentioned in the narrative bit but completely omitted from later mentions. M20 does this in spades, some things requiring you to jump all over the book before you get the complete picture.<br /> <br /> It's a terrible introduction to Mage, but as a compendium, it's not too bad.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This may be why I missed the entire combat section in my current book. I noticed it jumps around quite a bit. I will likely acquire both books, as it seems M20 gives tons of flavor information, and Revised seems to be easier to digest.<br /> <br /> Also, I'm my own boss, and spend most of my day driving from attorney office to attorney office, picking up and dropping off paperwork. All I think about as it is is Mage lol.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-07T20:18:57-07:00","id":1113074,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1113053#post1113053\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The beauty of Drive Thru RPG is that you can go both ways. You can buy just a PDF, get a book printed on demand, or even have them both. Of course, M20 being such a large book means that printing it will be expensive. So do what makes the most sense for your budget, keeping in mind that the PDF is the cheapest (and fastest, since you won't need to wait for the book to be delivered in the mail). The PDF is also the easiest to make copies from, since it's just a copy+paste away. I'd recommend only committing to the physical book if you like what you read in the PDF and are satisfied enough to make that investment.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> As a person who has never had the pleasure of GMing a game before, I can't personally say whether splitting the party is a good or bad idea. I'll leave that to the more experienced folk on the forum to field. That said, it certainly sounds more manageable to break up the large group into two smaller chunks. Each one can be focused on those present, giving players a greater time to shine and explore the roleplaying aspects of the game.<br /> <br /> It would also let you experiment more with the allegiances of the characters than you would otherwise. If the players are amenable, one group might be Tradition mages, while the other are Technocracy or Disparate ones. This would lend itself to an eventual confrontation, since they belong to different sides in the Ascension War. If you're handing out info from the book anyway, gauge how receptive certain players are to playing Technocrats or Disparates.<br /> <br /> Then again, circumstances may cause individuals from different sides to work together. Certain Disparate Crafts have been known to fraternize with Traditions that are similar, to the point that Revised actually folded a number of Crafts into the Traditions. M20 is metaplot agnostic by and large, so you could make this change in your setting true in whole, only in part (some members of a Craft went to the Traditions, while others remained independent), not true at all, or have membership overlap or be feigned (a Craft mage might play at being loyal to the Traditions, while secretly also working for the Disparate alliance). On the Technocrat side, agents of the Union might be working with Tradition mages for their own reasons - either against Union policy or under Union orders. They might even be spies and double agents within the sect's ranks, claiming membership in the Traditions while funneling intel to the Union and occasionally sabotaging Tradition efforts from within. As the characters grow closer, they may even be forced to examine where their priorities and loyalties lie: with the Union or with their friends. Defection or triple crosses may arise, if the players choose to pursue those avenues.<br /> <br /> On yet another hand, the two gaming groups may both be in the same sect, but be rivals. I'm imagining two Chantries, either in the same city or in neighboring cities, depending on how separated you want the groups to be in setting (at first).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I did just see on their site to order the physical copy of the book with a pdf is the same as just purchasing the book itself, so I'll probably pull the trigger on a hardback and PDF combo for revised. The M20 edition, though, will likely just get the PDF treatment haha.<br /> <br /> I'm thinking I'm going to present an opportunity to align with the Technocrats soon, as the characters have already spoken with a couple of them (I didn't emphasize those particular NPC's yet, I just made it known that they know these particular school faculty members). I don't know how it will go, as a few of my people like to play inherently &quot;good&quot; characters, and a couple of the others like to play what sounds like fun at the time. I won't make it an obvious choice, but I also won't present the Technocracy as a &quot;bad&quot; organization, because from what I've read lore-wise and such, they aren't necessarily &quot;bad&quot;, just have a different view of magick (although the overwhelming feeling of universal domination kind of makes it lean toward the bad side haha). Even if they all realize that's not the route to go, a batman &quot;choose between your lady or you district attorney&quot; scenario may give me an opportunity to split them at least temporarily.<br /> <br /> You guys rock, thanks again for fielding my silly questions. <br /> <br /> Side question: Anyone you reccomend, either on twitch or youtube or other avenues, worth watching who plays Mage? My tabletop experience is limited to my current group mostly, and watching nearly every episode of Critical Role with Matt Mercer, (and this is my first time really running any type of game as a primary ST/DM).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13642-the3obafett","name":"The3obaFett","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T01:40:07-07:00","id":1113151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The3obaFett</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1112358-new-member-with-some-probably-noobish-questions?p=1113031#post1113031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Good idea about having a chat with my power gamers. They will eventually figure out how to build some power toons, they always do in any game we play, hopefully they realize it takes the fun out of the game if they do. And if they don't, then, I will deal with it when that crossroad comes I guess lol.</div> </div> </div> </div>One of the advantages of Mage is that skills, attributes, knowledges and the like play a minor role when it comes to the potency of the Magick itself. They are merely windowdressing and describe ways of *how* the characters go about doing their magic.<br /> <br /> Other than that, a character's skill stats and the like are just that - mundane skills. And they should reflect a character's past life and current state after CG. 2 is average for many stats and skills for normal people. 3 is 'good / professional'. 4 is 'master'. 5 is pretty much legendary. And unlike D&amp;D, WoD does not have character 'levels', the requirement for planning ahead is very, very minimal compared to your previous game.<br /> Seriously. Try to get that into their heads. An average game session gives around 1-2 XP per RL session for long campaigns..more if it is supposed to be a fast-paced game or if long skips of time are involved. As in, ingame-years. The books have a good writeup about the XP distribution. And that XP can be immediately invested or saved up, but the increase in attributes, skills or magick spheres should *reflect what the character did use, train or is practicing in-game*<br /> <br /> The real power fireworks come from the selection of spheres. Mind that the max sphere level out of CG matches a character's Arete trait - which can have a max of 3. While it is possible to pull off powerful characters right out of chargen compared to a normal mortal, they are nothing compared to higher end Mages - unless they get to fire off their magick without consequence and requirements.<br /> <br /> Also: They cannot raise that Arete level post-chargen with just XP <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The character needs to go through a Seeking - a metaphysical trial and riddle, in a way - that is supposed to bring them enlightenment and realize a new step along their path to Ascension. An intricate roleplayed event happening either in a mindscape or reality. If they fail the seeking - tough taco. No increased Arete,.<br /> And a seeking happens at times the storyteller decides on, usually when the character had events happen that would push them towards that realization.<br /> <br /> So, even powergamers can't willy-nilly just pump their Arete up - you control the pace for that.<br /> <br /> Powergaming in mage though HEAVILY comes from abuse of the Spheres- which they can raise to Arete level, though that isn't the problem in itself.<br /> The problem comes from powergamers trying to ignore their character's paradigm and required tools, and pulling off magickal feats with the spheres that simply would not fit the kind of magickal training and belief that the character has. While trying to do it by merely snipping their fingers in-game.<br /> Essentially, the writeups of what each level of each sphere can do is the *whole* of the magickal toolbox - but not every character can pull all of it off simply because of their mindset of how magic works.<br /> Mage relies heavily on a dynamic of player self-restraint, and the ST keeping an eye on the same. It comes with the territory of a magic system that isn't made of fixed, pre-defined spells that you can buy, and a lack of mana bars.<br /> On the flipside of course, Mage is all about growing beyond your limits..","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 07-08-2017, 04:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T02:26:39-07:00","id":1113158,"title":"","content":"There is one RAW possible venue of raising Arete without a seeking: a Grimoire.<br /> <br /> But it must fit not only the Arete level you're after but also your Paradigm and possibly Practice","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T04:30:18-07:00","id":1113180,"title":"","content":"Also, if your players aren't awakened yet, then they have an arete rating of 0. That means no magic at all can be performed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-08T08:47:04-07:00","id":1113265,"title":"","content":"Yeah, powergaming could be a problem in this game. But there are ways to mitigate this somewhat.<br /> <br /> First, most of the really broken Sphere abilities are also the most Vulgar. So if you dole out Paradox points as normal and call for backlashes regularly (like whenever it seems most dramatic or just), the players will learn not to abuse them as much. That is, indeed, part of the reason Paradox exists as a game mechanic. Some Vulgar powers could be made coincidental under certain circumstances (turn invisible in a dark room, and it can't be proved to have happened by a Hypothetical Average Observer), but these tend to require some set-up or conditions being just right. As such, the amount of work or luck they need to do so can serve as their own moderating influences.<br /> <br /> Second, the most Vulgar, game-breaking Effects also tend to attract attention. And there's an entire sect - the Technocratic Union - for whom policing overt supernatural power is their entire job description. If you start turning cars into slag in the middle of Main Street, you're going to have MIBs and cyborgs knocking on your door.<br /> <br /> Indeed, this brings us to an important Third: Actions have Consequences. No use of magick occurs in a bubble; the Awakened arts have an effect on the world around them. More subtle uses of power will largely go unnoticed, but more dramatic alterations of Reality create larger ripples in the pond. So when a player pulls off an Effect, ask yourself what the possible ramifications of this action could result. If a player transforms into a tiger and runs around attacking people, it's going to make the evening news, and bring in organizations like Animal Control to search for the wayward big cat. If they use Forces 2 to change the traffic signals in their favor, it could potentially cause an accident. If they whip up a storm, it could devastate the area and potentially harm local infrastructure, depending on how intense the weather was.<br /> <br /> And if they go around killing a bunch of people, there WILL be police investigations. The weirder the deaths, the more intense the scrutiny. In DnD, many areas that adventurers go to are outside the rule of law (which usually gets concentrated around settlements and cities), and when the law is present, forensic science in the pseudo-medieval setting is less than stellar. In the World of Darkness, police are a thing that exists, and their methods are much more advanced. Even if the PCs kill in a manner that doesn't leave immediately damning evidence against them, there's going to be officers going around, asking for witnesses and checking alibis. Security footage will be reviewed where possible - remember also that modern people carry cameras with them everywhere, and are not afraid to document weird goings-on if they see it (making avoiding witnesses all the more essential). Not only police, but journalists will go around asking questions...as will the friends of anyone the PCs kill, as they understandably want answers and potentially revenge. Hunter groups form all the time from normal people who had loved ones fall victim to supernatural creatures, of which mages qualify. PCs that are too sloppy with their murder-hobo tendencies could become the targets of angry witch-hunters.<br /> <br /> Fourth, keep in mind also that forces in the material world aren't the only ones that affect - and are affected by - that material world. Spirits existing in the Umbra can take an interest in the activities of mages that abuse their magick. Elemental Effects might attract elemental spirits. Ghosts feed on emotions, and so manipulating them in the living could attract the dead. Whether the spirits dislike how the mages are upsetting the natural order - or whether they like it, and want to be around them at all times - either way can lead to problems for the mages. And the bigger the Effects, the more they attract spirits, for good and ill.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}